\[
P(O)+P(B)=1-0.8=0.2.
\]

This only tells us that the orange and blackcurrant probabilities add to \(0.2\), but it does not tell us how that \(0.2\) is split between them. For example, \(P(O)\) could be \(0.2\), \(0.1\), \(0\), or many other values.

Therefore, there is not enough information to determine the probability of choosing an orange sweet. The correct answer is Impossible to know.

