Correct-answer row 808 | question_id=1398
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
question:
Which of the following is most likely to be the graph of a reciprocal function?

correct answer:
![A graph with two curves, in the top right and bottom left quadrants. The top right curves starts up and right of the origin and travels steeply down and right, it then levels off to travel alongside the x-axis without touching it. The bottom left curve starts down and left of the origin, travels steeply up and left, it then levels off just below the x-axis without touching it.]()

generated reasoning trace:
A reciprocal function has the general shape \(y=\frac{k}{x}\). Its graph has two separate branches and two asymptotes: the \(x\)-axis and the \(y\)-axis. The branches never touch either axis.

If \(k>0\), the branches lie in Quadrants I and III. In Quadrant I, the curve drops steeply near the \(y\)-axis and then levels off toward the \(x\)-axis. In Quadrant III, the curve rises steeply near the \(y\)-axis and then levels off toward the \(x\)-axis from below.

So the most likely graph is the one with two curves in the top right and bottom left quadrants, each approaching but never touching the axes.

The correct answer is: ![A graph with two curves, in the top right and bottom left quadrants. The top right curves starts up and right of the origin and travels steeply down and right, it then levels off to travel alongside the x-axis without touching it. The bottom left curve starts down and left of the origin, travels steeply up and left, it then levels off just below the x-axis without touching it.]()

