question:
One of these quadratic equations will give exactly one solution.
Which one?

correct answer:
\( \frac{-3 \pm \sqrt{0}}{5} \)

generated reasoning trace:
A quadratic has exactly one real solution when its discriminant is zero, so in the quadratic formula
\[
x=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a},
\]
we need the part under the square root to be \(0\).

Among the choices, the expression
\[
\frac{-3\pm\sqrt{0}}{5}
\]
has discriminant \(0\), so the \(\pm\) does not create two different values:
\[
\frac{-3+\sqrt{0}}{5}=\frac{-3}{5}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\frac{-3-\sqrt{0}}{5}=\frac{-3}{5}.
\]
So it gives exactly one solution.

The correct answer is \( \frac{-3 \pm \sqrt{0}}{5} \).

