question:
Square \( \mathrm{P} \) has an area of \( 4 \mathrm{~cm}^{2} \)
Square \( Q \) has an area of \( 4 \mathrm{~mm}^{2} \)
What is the total area of squares \( P \) and \( Q \) in \( \mathrm{mm}^{2} \) ?

target distractor:
\( 40004 \mathrm{~mm}^{2} \)

misconception:
Thinks there are 100mm in a centimetre

generated reasoning trace:
Square \(P\) has area \(4\text{ cm}^2\) and square \(Q\) has area \(4\text{ mm}^2\).

I need both areas in \(\text{mm}^2\), so I convert \(4\text{ cm}^2\) to \(\text{mm}^2\).

Since there are \(100\) mm in \(1\) cm, I do:
\[
1\text{ cm}^2 = 100 \times 100 = 10000\text{ mm}^2
\]

So:
\[
4\text{ cm}^2 = 4 \times 10000 = 40000\text{ mm}^2
\]

Now add the area of square \(Q\):
\[
40000 + 4 = 40004\text{ mm}^2
\]

So the total area is \(\boxed{40004\text{ mm}^2}\).

